---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import Gloss from '../../components/Gloss.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar, line } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const six = assertion('six-per-cent-of-the-county-is-floodplain')
const band = assertion('the-five-hundred-year-band-is-a-sixteenth')
const zoneA = assertion('one-acre-in-seven-has-no-elevation')
const panels = assertion('the-flood-map-is-three-maps')
const wet = assertion('wet-ground-and-flood-zone-are-not-the-same-ground')
const four = assertion('four-villages-have-no-floodplain-at-all')
const gomer = assertion('gomer-is-a-third-floodplain')
const howmany = assertion('how-many-people-live-in-the-floodplain')
const rain = assertion('more-rain-than-river')
const tidal = assertion('tidal-water-five-hundred-miles-from-the-sea')
const bluffton = assertion('a-village-of-four-thousand-holds-a-third-of-the-money')
const june = assertion('june-2015-and-nobody-declared-it')
const ten = assertion('ten-claim-years-and-one-declaration')
const repeat = assertion('twenty-seven-buildings-and-one-buyout')
const half = assertion('half-the-claims-were-rated-outside-the-map')
const ratio = assertion('the-ratio-is-about-who-had-to-buy')

const tw = ['Marion', 'Sugar Creek', 'Spencer', 'Monroe', 'Perry', 'Amanda', 'Richland', 'Auglaize', 'American', 'Shawnee', 'Jackson', 'Bath', 'Lima']
const twHydric = [58.0, 48.0, 45.2, 44.3, 34.9, 34.1, 33.8, 32.0, 30.0, 26.2, 23.4, 21.1, 13.4]
const twFlood = [5.73, 17.04, 2.63, 3.34, 7.52, 7.35, 4.92, 0.81, 8.77, 6.51, 4.87, 7.13, 4.27]

const cy = [1978, 1979, 1980, 1981, 1982, 1983, 1984, 1985, 1986, 1987, 1988, 1989, 1990, 1991, 1992, 1993, 1994, 1995, 1996, 1997, 1998, 1999, 2000, 2001, 2002, 2003, 2004, 2005, 2006, 2007, 2008, 2009, 2010, 2011, 2012, 2013, 2014, 2015, 2016, 2017, 2018, 2019, 2020, 2021, 2022, 2023]
const cn = [3, 10, 2, 26, 4, 0, 1, 0, 4, 0, 1, 1, 2, 0, 9, 0, 0, 0, 0, 10, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 2, 3, 1, 25, 10, 7, 0, 33, 0, 5, 1, 47, 3, 11, 3, 27, 2, 1, 2, 2]
const cp = [0, 11, 5, 122, 8, 0, 0, 0, 16, 0, 0, 3, 7, 0, 28, 0, 0, 0, 0, 56, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 3, 0, 0, 872, 390, 46, 0, 545, 0, 129, 15, 1244, 28, 11, 28, 523, 3, 1, 4, 8]

const wetSpec = line(
  tw,
  [
    { name: 'hydric soil', y: twHydric },
    { name: 'mapped floodplain', y: twFlood },
  ],
  { yTitle: 'Per cent of the township', tickSuffix: '%', zeroBased: true, labelLast: true },
)

const claimSpec = line(
  cy,
  [
    { name: 'claims', y: cn },
  ],
  { yTitle: 'Flood insurance claims', zeroBased: true },
)

const paidSpec = line(
  cy,
  [
    { name: 'paid, thousands of dollars', y: cp },
  ],
  { yTitle: 'Paid, thousands of dollars', zeroBased: true },
)

const causeSpec = bar(
  ['rainfall or snowmelt', 'stream, river or lake', 'tidal water', 'other causes', 'alluvial fan'],
  [109, 103, 27, 15, 1],
  { yTitle: 'Claims', labelValues: true },
)
---

<Article slug="the-map-is-of-the-river">
  <p>
    Allen County is three-quarters poorly drained. It was settled late because the ground held water,
    it was made farmable by ditch and tile, and its wettest township is 58 per cent hydric soil.
    None of that is what the flood map is about. The federal map traces channels, and this is the
    first time the corpus has held both it and the record of where water actually got into a
    building.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={six} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={band} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={zoneA} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={panels} />

  <h2>Where the map puts it</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={wet} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="wet"
    title="The wettest ground is not the mapped ground"
    caption="Townships in order of hydric soil, highest first, with the share of each inside the special flood hazard area beside it. Marion has the most waterlogged ground in the county and the seventh most floodplain; Sugar Creek is second on soil and first on flood zone. The two rank-orders correlate at a Spearman 0.099."
    columns={['Township', 'Hydric soil', 'Floodplain']}
    rows={tw.map((t, i) => [t, `${twHydric[i]}%`, `${twFlood[i]}%`])}
    height={340}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="wet" set:html={JSON.stringify(wetSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={four} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={gomer} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={howmany} />
  <Gloss>
    The bracket is the honest answer and it is uselessly wide: 235 people in the blocks that lie
    wholly inside the hazard area, 23,721 in every block that touches it, and 4,448 if each block is
    weighted by the share of its area inside — which is the weight this corpus has already
    established is the wrong one for people. A census block is a unit drawn for counting people, not
    for following a watercourse, and 755 of the 849 blocks that meet the floodplain are cut by its
    edge. Closing this needs building footprints.
  </Gloss>

  <h2>Where the water went</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={rain} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="cause"
    title="More claims from rain than from rivers"
    caption="Cause of loss on the 259 flood insurance claims paid in Allen County since 1978, using the programme's own code list. Accumulation of rainfall or snowmelt is the largest single cause."
    columns={['Cause', 'Claims']}
    rows={[
      ['accumulation of rainfall or snowmelt', '109'],
      ['stream, river or lake overflow', '103'],
      ['tidal water overflow', '27'],
      ['other causes', '15'],
      ['alluvial fan overflow', '1'],
    ]}
    height={300}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="cause" set:html={JSON.stringify(causeSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={tidal} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="claims"
    title="Forty-six years of claims, in five spikes"
    caption="Flood insurance claims paid in Allen County by year of loss. Twenty-three of the forty-six years have none at all. 2015 is the tallest at 47, and 1981 at 26 is the tallest before 2007."
    columns={['Year', 'Claims', 'Paid ($000)']}
    rows={cy.filter((_, i) => cn[i] > 0).map((y) => {
      const i = cy.indexOf(y)
      return [y, cn[i], cp[i].toLocaleString()]
    })}
    height={300}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="claims" set:html={JSON.stringify(claimSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={bluffton} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={june} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="paid"
    title="Thirty per cent of the money is one month"
    caption="Paid on flood insurance claims in Allen County by year of loss, in thousands of dollars, unadjusted. June 2015 accounts for forty-five of that year's forty-seven claims and for 30.3 per cent of everything paid here since 1978."
    columns={['Year', 'Paid ($000)']}
    rows={cy.filter((_, i) => cp[i] > 0).map((y) => [y, cp[cy.indexOf(y)].toLocaleString()])}
    height={300}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="paid" set:html={JSON.stringify(paidSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={ten} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={repeat} />

  <h2>The comparison that will not hold</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={half} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={ratio} />
  <Gloss>
    Inside the special flood hazard area a federally backed mortgage requires flood insurance;
    outside it nobody is required to buy anything. So the policies inside are close to every
    mortgaged building on that ground and the policies outside are whoever thought it worth paying
    for — which is the households that have watched water stand in their street. The rate is exact
    and the ratio between two rates recruited that differently is about the recruitment. What
    survives without a denominator is the cause of loss, and it says the same thing more quietly:
    the water that gets into a building here is as often water that had nowhere to go.
  </Gloss>
</Article>
